From ccae518facefbb8ee5ea77cd44e7fd8e26bc3a5a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vincenzo Grimaldi <vince.ceccarelli@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 9 Sep 2026 19:45:36 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 17/17] feat(mcp): the engines as callable tools, and a
 machine-readable surface
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Being found gets a page mentioned. Being callable gets it used. Every
engine here was already correct and already documented for people; none of
it was reachable by a model that wanted to run a calculation rather than
read about one.

An MCP server at /mcp

  Streamable HTTP, JSON-RPC 2.0, protocol revision 2025-06-18. Eight
  read-only tools: economics, eligibility, metering concept, allocation,
  billing, the quarter-hour grid, market identifiers, MSCONS. Single
  requests and batches; notifications answered 202 with no body; GET
  declined with 405 because this server never initiates messages, so a
  stream would cost a client a connection for nothing.

  server/api-v1.ts is refactored so each endpoint is a pure handler over a
  ResultSink, and dispatchApiV1() runs those handlers in process. MCP does
  not call the API over HTTP and does not reimplement it — both surfaces
  execute the same function. e2e/mcp.spec.ts asserts that for all eight
  tools by comparing normalised responses byte for byte, so a change that
  made them diverge fails rather than ships.

  Refusals travel as tool results, not protocol errors: 96 values on the
  100-interval day comes back isError with unsupported_input and the reason,
  which an agent can read and correct. The instructions returned by
  initialize state the three things that are true here and guessed
  everywhere else — the grid is 92/96/100, a refusal is a correct answer,
  and nothing here rules on legal admissibility.

Schemas that cannot drift from the validator

  toJsonSchema() was hand-written against Zod 3 and threw on every tool
  under Zod 4; tools/list was unusable. It now delegates to Zod's own
  converter and asserts the result is an object schema. The published
  OpenAPI request bodies, the GET query parameters and the tool list in
  /.well-known/mcp.json are all generated from those same schemas, so the
  spec an agent reads is the contract the server enforces.

  The build scripts no longer scrape TypeScript with regular expressions.
  scripts/load-manifests.mjs bundles the real modules with esbuild and
  executes them, which removes the failure where a reformatted manifest
  silently publishes something other than what the server runs.

/mcp was routed at the page shell in production

  vercel.json rewrote only /api/* to the function, so /mcp would have
  returned index.html with status 200 to an MCP client — a failure that
  passes every local test. It is now routed at the server and excluded from
  caches, and client/src/lib/deployment.test.ts evaluates the rewrite rules
  against every endpoint so the next one cannot be missed.

Answers a model can quote

  shared/faq.ts holds the questions this codebase can answer precisely,
  with the answers: the clock-change grid and why an annual total proves
  nothing, what decides a metering concept, why the three allocation keys
  differ, what the API refuses and why. They render on the page, they are
  emitted as FAQPage structured data, and they are inlined into
  /llms-full.txt. Tests assert no answer claims legal admissibility and no
  answer cites a statute, because no citation here has been verified.

  Documents other than the landing page were copying the landing page's
  FAQPage and HowTo, asserting one @id at several URLs. Page-level entities
  now belong to their own page. /rechner serves the landing page under a
  second URL; it is marked as an alias, points its canonical at the page it
  duplicates and leaves the sitemap, instead of competing with it.

  robots.txt names nineteen AI crawlers explicitly and allows them.
  robots.txt now has one owner: two scripts were writing it, and the richer
  version survived only because of the order they happened to run in.

  A documented Messlokations-ID in the API reference was 31 characters
  where the engine requires 33 — the first example a developer copies was
  one the engine refuses. Fixed, with a test over every documented example.

567 unit tests, 468 end-to-end across three viewports.
